     ]
    },
    {
     "t": 100.309,
     "d": 7.901,
     "txt": "In two thousand four, three researchers, Avi Assor, Guy Roth and Edward Deci, asked college students about growing up.",
     "v": {
      "dir": {
       "k": "nomes",
       "t": [
        "Avi Assor",
        "Guy Roth",
        "Edward Deci"
       ],
       "s": "JOURNAL OF PERSONALITY \u00b7 2004"
      }
     },
     "dest": [
      "researchers"
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": 108.71,
     "d": 4.13,
     "txt": "Did your parents give you more affection when you did well? And less when you didn't?",
     "v": {
      "dir": {
       "k": "selo",
       "t": [
        "MORE LOVE",
        "WHEN YOU WIN?"
       ]
      }
     },
     "dest": [
      "more",
      "less"
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": 113.34,
     "d": 5.75,
     "txt": "The students who said yes had something in common. It had worked. They did what their parents wanted.",
     "v": {
      "dir": {
       "k": "selo",
       "t": [
        "IT WORKED.",
        "THEY PERFORMED."
       ]
      }
     },
     "dest": [
      "worked"
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": 119.59,
     "d": 4.962,
     "txt": "But they described it as pressure from the inside. Not, I want to. I have to.",
     "v": {
      "dir": {
       "k": "selo",
       "t": [
        "NOT \u201cI WANT TO.\u201d",
        "\u201cI HAVE TO.\u201d"
       ]
      }
     },
     "dest": [
      "have",
      "to"
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": 125.052,
     "d": 7.166,
     "txt": "Their good feelings after a success didn't last. Their shame after a failure did. And they resented their parents for it.",
     "v": {
      "dir": {
       "k": "itens",
       "cab": [
        "What it cost them:"
       ],
       "n": 3,
       "auto": 1,
       "it": [
        "Pride that faded fast",
        "Shame after failing",
        "Resentment toward parents"
       ]
      }
     },
     "dest": [
      "shame"
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": 132.718,
     "d": 5.524,
     "txt": "Then the researchers looked at mothers and their grown daughters. And found the pattern handed down.",
     "v": {
      "dir": {
       "k": "selo",
       "t": [
        "MOTHERS.",
        "DAUGHTERS."
       ]
      }
     },
     "dest": [
      "handed",
      "down"
     ]
    },
    {
     "t": 138.742,
     "d": 8.813,
     "txt": "Mothers who had been loved conditionally as girls felt worse about themselves as adults. And their daughters described being raised the very same way.",
     "v": {
      "rod": "HANDED DOWN, MOTHER TO DAUGHTER."
     },
     "dest": [
      "same",
      "way"
     ]
    }
   ]
